```javascript
// var - function scoped, can be redeclared
var name = 'John';
var name = 'Jane'; // OK
function example() {
  var x = 1;
  if (true) {
    var x = 2; // Same variable
  }
  console.log(x); // 2
}

// let - block scoped, can be reassigned
let age = 25;
age = 26; // OK
if (true) {
  let age = 30; // Different variable
}

// const - block scoped, cannot be reassigned
const PI = 3.14159;
// PI = 3.14; // Error!
const user = { name: 'John' };
user.name = 'Jane'; // OK - object properties can change
```

questions.backOfCard:
**Explanation:**
• var has function scope and can be redeclared
• let has block scope and can be reassigned
• const has block scope but cannot be reassigned

**Usage:**
```javascript
// var - function scoped
var name = 'John';
var name = 'Jane'; // OK

// let - block scoped, reassignable
let age = 25;
age = 26; // OK

// const - block scoped, not reassignable
const PI = 3.14159;
const user = { name: 'John' };
user.name = 'Jane'; // OK - properties can change
```

**Real World Example:**
E-commerce app: const for API_URL config, let for cart counts, avoid var to prevent scoping bugs in checkout.

