```javascript
// Prototypal inheritance
const animal = {
  eat() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is eating`);
  }
};

const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.name = 'Buddy';
dog.bark = function() {
  console.log(`${this.name} is barking`);
};

dog.eat(); // Buddy is eating
dog.bark(); // Buddy is barking

// Classical inheritance (ES6 classes)
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  eat() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is eating`);
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  bark() {
    console.log(`${this.name} is barking`);
  }
}

const buddy = new Dog('Buddy');
buddy.eat(); // Buddy is eating
buddy.bark(); // Buddy is barking
```

questions.backOfCard:
**Explanation:**
• Prototypal: objects inherit directly from other objects via prototype chain
• Classical: uses classes as blueprints (ES6+)
• Prototypal is more flexible, classical is more structured

**Usage:**
```javascript
// Prototypal
const animal = { eat() { console.log('eating'); } };
const dog = Object.create(animal);
dog.bark = function() { console.log('barking'); };

// Classical
class Animal { eat() { console.log('eating'); } }
class Dog extends Animal { bark() { console.log('barking'); } }
```

**Real World Example:**
Social media: User objects use prototypal for flexibility, UI components use classical for structured hierarchy (BaseComponent -> Button).

